C MP+scoped-relaxed
"Heterogeneous message passing under HRF. The producer and consumer each issue a
 release/acquire scoped to their OWN work-group, so they never synchronise at a
 shared scope. The genuine HRF-style source is:

   // producer, work-group A
   x = 1;
   atomic_store(&f, 1, release, scope(A));     // scope A only
   // consumer, work-group B
   r0 = atomic_load(&f, acquire, scope(B));    // scope B only
   r1 = x;

 HRF generalises DRF to a scope hierarchy: two synchronising operations order each
 other only when they name a COMMON scope. Here they do not, so x is touched by a
 heterogeneous (scoped) data race and HRF gives no SC-like guarantee — the MP
 outcome is allowed, exactly as for relaxed accesses (modelled here with relaxed
 atomics, herd7 -c11 => Sometimes). Flat C11 has a single global scope, so the
 same release/acquire DO synchronise and the outcome is forbidden (MP+relacq.litmus).
 Choosing a common scope (e.g. device) for both recovers the C11 guarantee."
{ int x=0; int f=0; }
P0 (atomic_int* x, atomic_int* f) {
  atomic_store_explicit(x,1,memory_order_relaxed);
  atomic_store_explicit(f,1,memory_order_relaxed);
}
P1 (atomic_int* x, atomic_int* f) {
  int r0 = atomic_load_explicit(f,memory_order_relaxed);
  int r1 = atomic_load_explicit(x,memory_order_relaxed);
}
exists (1:r0=1 /\ 1:r1=0)
